Yes — you were remembering it correctly, with one important distinction: the 80% goal was established by the Commission created by Clinton’s executive order, rather than being stated directly in the executive order itself.

The executive order

Executive Order 13015 — “White House Commission on Aviation Safety and Security”

* President: William J. Clinton
* Signed: August 22, 1996
* Federal Register: 61 FR 43937, August 27, 1996
* Chair: Vice President Al Gore
* Purpose: Establish a commission to develop a strategy for improving aviation safety and security, including air traffic control. 

Read the full Executive Order 13015 in the Federal Register⁠￼

The order itself does not contain the 80% figure. Instead, it created the commission and tasked it with developing a safety strategy. 

Where the “80% in 10 years” challenge came from

The White House Commission on Aviation Safety and Security, commonly called the Gore Commission, issued its final report on February 12, 1997.

One of its central recommendations was a national goal of reducing the commercial aviation fatal accident rate by 80% within 10 years—i.e., by approximately 2007. The FAA explicitly describes the report as challenging the FAA and industry to accomplish this reduction. 

FAA copy of the White House Commission's final report⁠￼

Clinton subsequently publicly endorsed the goal when he received the report. In his February 12, 1997 remarks, he said the administration was working to “cut the fatal accident rate by 80 percent” and specifically discussed the FAA, airline industry, and NASA working together toward the goal. 

The sequence was therefore:

August 22, 1996
→ Clinton signs EO 13015, creating the White House Commission on Aviation Safety and Security. 

February 12, 1997
→ Gore Commission delivers its final report, establishing/recommending the 80% reduction in fatal accident rate over 10 years goal. 

1998
→ FAA and industry launch the Safer Skies Initiative, turning the goal into a systematic, data-driven safety program. 

2008
→ FAA later reported that the 10-year goal was achieved—and actually exceeded, with an 84% reduction in fatality risk over the period. 

So if you’re looking for the legal/presidential document that started this chain of events, it’s definitely Executive Order 13015. If you’re looking for the document that actually articulated the famous 80%-in-10-years aviation safety challenge, it’s the February 12, 1997 Final Report of the White House Commission on Aviation Safety and Security.

And interestingly, this is the origin of the Commercial Aviation Safety Team (CAST) approach you were asking about previously: the 80% goal led directly into the government/industry partnership and data-driven safety methodology that became CAST. 